---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Ball Valve Actuators | Floating & Trunnion Automation | PNEUMACTUATOR"
  description="Pneumatic actuators for ball valve automation: rack and pinion for floating ball valves, scotch yoke for trunnion-mounted high pressure valves, spring return fail-safe options across ANSI and PN pressure classes."
>
  <section class="bg-navy-950 text-white">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-night-green">INDUSTRIES · BALL VALVES</p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight">Ball Valve Automation with Pneumatic Actuators</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-lg text-slate-300">On-off and modulating actuation for floating and trunnion-mounted ball valves across ANSI and PN pressure classes, with spring return fail-safe options for isolation and ESD duty.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-white">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 py-16 sm:px-6">
      <!-- 应用背景 -->
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-blue">APPLICATION BACKGROUND</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">Automating Ball Valves in Pipelines and Process Plants</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">A ball valve gives tight shutoff with a quarter turn of the stem, and the ball bore leaves the flow path unobstructed when open. Compared with gate valves of the same size, ball valves need less torque to operate and seal reliably even in media that carries solids or deposits. Those properties make them a first choice for pipelines, process plants, energy facilities and marine systems, and pneumatic actuation brings remote operation, fast cycling and fail-safe behavior to the valve.</p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">The two main constructions are floating ball and trunnion-mounted. In a floating ball valve, upstream pressure pushes the ball against the downstream seat to create the seal. Floating designs are common up to roughly DN200–DN300 and ANSI Class 150/300 duty, typically with soft seats such as PTFE or reinforced PTFE, and their torque requirements stay modest. In a trunnion-mounted valve, the ball is supported top and bottom, so seat loads are controlled and the design extends to larger sizes and higher pressure classes — ANSI 600 and above are routine — with metal or soft seats and higher torque demand. Pressure class, ball surface finish and whether the media lubricates the seats all move the required torque.</p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">For on-off service, breakaway torque — the torque needed to unseat the ball — is usually the peak value the actuator must cover. That is one reason scotch yoke actuators appear so often on ball valves: the yoke mechanism delivers rising output torque toward the ends of the stroke, which lines up with the high torque demand at the open and closed positions. Floating ball valves in smaller sizes are handled comfortably by rack and pinion units, and a positioner with feedback extends either family to modulating duty on V-port and control ball valves.</p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Speed is another selection factor. Pneumatic actuators cycle in seconds rather than minutes, which suits loading, isolation and interlock duties where a fast response is required. Stroke time depends on actuator size, air supply volume and the solenoid or control valve used — larger actuators and spring return units generally need more air and therefore take longer unless an auxiliary volume tank or quick-exhaust valve is added. Where a control loop positions the ball valve continuously, the positioner's resolution and the actuator's friction characteristics determine how smoothly the valve tracks the setpoint, so matching the positioner to the actuator size is part of building a stable loop. Cycle rate also feeds into the choice: frequent cycling favors actuators with sealed, lubricated drive trains, while valves that sit closed for months still need the breakaway torque covered on demand.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- 推荐产品表 -->
      <div class="mt-16">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-blue">RECOMMENDED ACTUATORS</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">Actuator Families for Ball Valve Duty</h2>
        <div class="mt-6 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
            <thead class="bg-navy-950 text-white">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-6 py-3.5 font-semibold">Actuator family</th>
                <th class="px-6 py-3.5 font-semibold">Typical ball valve duty</th>
                <th class="px-6 py-3.5 font-semibold">When it fits</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100">
              <tr class="bg-white">
                <td class="px-6 py-3 font-medium text-navy-950"><a href="/products/rack-and-pinion/" class="text-brand-blue hover:underline">Rack and pinion</a></td>
                <td class="px-6 py-3 text-slate-600">Floating ball valves, small to mid sizes</td>
                <td class="px-6 py-3 text-slate-600">Compact on-off and modulating actuation with fast cycling</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50/60">
                <td class="px-6 py-3 font-medium text-navy-950"><a href="/products/spring-return/" class="text-brand-blue hover:underline">Rack and pinion spring return</a></td>
                <td class="px-6 py-3 text-slate-600">Floating ball valves with fail-safe requirement</td>
                <td class="px-6 py-3 text-slate-600">Fail-close or fail-open when air supply is lost</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-white">
                <td class="px-6 py-3 font-medium text-navy-950"><a href="/products/scotch-yoke/" class="text-brand-blue hover:underline">Scotch yoke</a></td>
                <td class="px-6 py-3 text-slate-600">Trunnion-mounted valves, higher pressure classes</td>
                <td class="px-6 py-3 text-slate-600">High torque at stroke ends matching breakaway demand; economical in large sizes</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-50/60">
                <td class="px-6 py-3 font-medium text-navy-950"><a href="/products/scotch-yoke/" class="text-brand-blue hover:underline">Scotch yoke spring return</a></td>
                <td class="px-6 py-3 text-slate-600">Large or safety-critical ball valves</td>
                <td class="px-6 py-3 text-slate-600">Fail-safe with high output torque on big bore valves</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-slate-500">PNEUMACTUATOR supplies double acting and spring return actuators across 8 Nm to 200,000 Nm output torque. Final model selection follows the valve's torque curve at your operating pressure.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- 关键考虑点 -->
      <div class="mt-16">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-blue">KEY CONSIDERATIONS</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">What to Check When Automating a Ball Valve</h2>
        <ul class="mt-6 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <li class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-700"><span class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Breakaway torque:</span> usually the sizing peak for on-off ball valves; confirm it against the valve torque curve, including the effect of line pressure.</li>
          <li class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-700"><span class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Seat and media:</span> soft seats (PTFE family) versus metal seats, and whether the media lubricates — both shift operating torque.</li>
          <li class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-700"><span class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Pressure class:</span> operating pressure versus test pressure changes the torque the actuator must handle in service.</li>
          <li class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-700"><span class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Supply air:</span> output torque scales with air pressure; dry, filtered air keeps performance stable.</li>
          <li class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-700"><span class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Fail-safe position:</span> spring return to close or open per plant safety logic, for isolation and ESD services.</li>
          <li class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-700"><span class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Interface:</span> ISO 5211 mounting and NAMUR accessory pattern for direct installation of solenoid, limit switch or positioner.</li>
          <li class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 p-4 text-sm text-slate-700"><span class="font-semibold text-navy-950">Modulating duty:</span> V-port and control ball valves need a positioner with feedback and an actuator chosen for frequent positioning cycles.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <div class="mt-16">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-blue">FAQ</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy-950 sm:text-3xl">Ball Valve Actuation Questions</h2>
        <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-950">Floating or trunnion ball valve — which actuator do I need?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Size and pressure class decide it. Floating ball valves in small to mid sizes and ANSI Class 150/300 duty are handled well by rack and pinion actuators. Trunnion-mounted valves at large sizes or ANSI 600 and above pull higher breakaway torque, so a scotch yoke, with or without spring return, is the usual fit. Confirm the torque at your operating pressure before selecting.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-950">Why does a scotch yoke suit ball valves?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">A scotch yoke's output torque rises toward the ends of the stroke, and that is exactly where ball valves demand the most torque — at breakaway and at reseating. The same mechanism delivers high torque in a more compact package than an equivalent rack and pinion unit, which matters on large bore and high pressure ball valves.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-950">When is spring return required on a ball valve actuator?</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600">Whenever the valve must reach a defined position when the air supply fails — fail-closed for isolation of a line, fail-open where continued flow is the safe state. Spring return also serves ESD logic on pipelines and process plants. The fail position is set by the plant's safety requirements, not by the actuator alone.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    title="Need a Ball Valve Actuator Package?"
    subtitle="Tell us the valve configuration, size, pressure class and fail-safe position — we recommend the right rack and pinion or scotch yoke package within 24 hours. Email sales@pneumactuator.com."
  />
</Layout>
